<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vojvodina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vojvodina is an autonomous province of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, covering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/backa">Bačka</a>, the Serbian part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Srem</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novi-sad">Novi Sad</a>. Long part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands, it took its name from the Serbian Vojvodina proclaimed in 1848. It joined the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> in 1918 and remains ethnically mixed, with a large Hungarian minority.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vojvodina.jpg" alt="Vojvodina" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of Vojvodina · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Vojvodina.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom united some twelve million people in 1921, rising to about sixteen million by 1941, from regions with very different pasts: former Habsburg Slovenia, Croatia, <b>Vojvodina</b> and Bosnia-Herzegovina, and the formerly Ottoman lands of Serbia, Montenegro, Kosovo and Macedonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The six republics, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Croatia, Macedonia, Montenegro, Serbia and Slovenia, were constitutionally equal; Serbia alone contained two autonomous provinces, Kosovo and <b>Vojvodina</b>, which the 1974 constitution gave voting power equal to the republics', to the lasting anger of Serbian officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Serbian tradition remembers him as a forerunner of later Serbian autonomy in <b>Vojvodina</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/realm-of-jovan-nenad">Realm of Jovan Nenad</a></li><li><blockquote>A national assembly at Sremski Karlovci in May 1848 proclaimed the Serbian <b>Vojvodina</b> in Srem, Baranya, Bačka and Banat, elected Josif Rajačić patriarch and Colonel Stevan Šupljikac voivode, and set up a Main Board led by Đorđe Stratimirović.</blockquote><a href="/state/serbian-vojvodina">Serbian Vojvodina</a></li><li><blockquote>On 29 October the Croatian Sabor broke ties with Hungary and Austria, and the new state was proclaimed, covering Slovenia, Croatia-Slavonia, Dalmatia, Bosnia and Herzegovina and <b>Vojvodina</b>, with the Slovene politician Anton Korošec as president.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a></li><li><blockquote>The name survived in the modern Serbian province of <b>Vojvodina</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/voivodeship-of-serbia-and-banat-of-temeschwar">Voivodeship of Serbia and Banat of Temeschwar</a></li></ul></section>
